---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About — Ciphriend">
  <article class="prose-style max-w-2xl space-y-6">
    <header>
      <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary mb-2">
        &#47;&#47; about
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-display tracking-tight">A friendly cipher tool.</h1>
    </header>

    <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
      Ciphriend is a small web tool for encoding, decoding, and visualizing ciphers — from classical substitutions to modern crypto primitives. It's named after the two things it tries to be at once: <span class="text-foreground">cipher</span> and <span class="text-foreground">friend</span>.
    </p>

    <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
      Every operation runs in your browser. No server, no telemetry, no logging. The architecture is the privacy promise — we can't peek at your inputs because we never see them.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-foreground mb-2">
        Visualization
      </h2>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
        Educational ciphers come with a per-character visualization showing what the cipher is actually doing. The substitution-style ones (Caesar, ROT13, Atbash, Vigenère) share a generic visualizer; ciphers with a unique shape (Rail Fence, Base64, Morse, frequency analysis) get bespoke ones.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-foreground mb-2">
        Open source
      </h2>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
        Built with Astro and Svelte. <a href="https://github.com/joshloredo/ciphriend" class="text-primary hover:underline">Source on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
